    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d224">

                <pb facs="0309" n="205" sameAs="frus:frus1932v03/pg205"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/4014/y</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Commander in Chief, United States Asiatic Fleet
                        (Taylor), to the Chief of Naval Operations (Pratt)</hi><anchor n="51"
                        xml:id="d224-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Shanghai</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-02-04T13:39:00+08:00">February 4, 1932—1:39
                        p.m.</date>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Japanese posts and patrols have been withdrawn from British and American sectors.
                    This gives control of mills and prevents their use to display panels for
                    aircraft and eliminates danger which Chinese feared of flank attack. This
                    greatly eases situation. Woosung forts again under bombardment from cruisers and
                    destroyers. No planes in evidence. Weather unfavorable.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
